This plugin bundles reference data and four overview pictures of the map, and downloads the detailed map pictures from its own
GitHub release (see Map imagery). The imagery and data were gathered once, at build time, by the plugin's build tools, which
are kept outside this repository, and are refreshed only when a new release is made. The
version of the wiki's map that the tiles were taken from is recorded in maps.json, in the plugin's jar. This file is
also inside the jar, as META-INF/NOTICE, next to META-INF/LICENSE.

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Map imagery
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
What is bundled and what is downloaded: the map tiles of the game's maps (the surface, its floors, and the dungeon and other
maps) are downloaded, the first time an area is viewed, from the plugin's own GitHub release
(https://github.com/KingKj52/world-map-plus-maps) and kept on the player's computer. Only files of that release are
fetched, each checked against a SHA-256, and GitHub sees the player's IP address, as for any download. Four overview pictures
made by averaging the tiles are bundled in the jar.

Where from: the Old School RuneScape Wiki's map server (https://maps.runescape.wiki/osrs/), fetched once at
build time. The wiki's page RuneScape:Map (https://oldschool.runescape.wiki/w/RuneScape:Map) says the tiles are
made with a modified version of RuneLite's MapImageDumper, which draws them from the game cache.

Licence: the tiles are pictures of the game's own map, so they are content of Jagex Limited, used here under
Jagex's Fan Content Policy as the credit above says. The wiki's footer says its content is under CC BY-NC-SA 3.0
with additional terms, but the page above states no licence for the tiles, and the wiki's licensing page
(https://meta.weirdgloop.org/w/Meta:Copyrights) says that non-text media on its wikis should not be assumed to be
under the same licence as the text. For that reason this plugin does not claim that the tiles are under
CC BY-NC-SA, and does not claim any licence for them. The BSD 2-Clause licence of this plugin covers its own
source code only. The tiles are credited to the wiki as the place they were taken from and to Jagex as their
owner. The plugin is free and non-commercial.

What was changed: the tiles are PNG files grouped into packs (zip files without compression) for download, the overview
pictures are averaged from them, and a few map squares that the wiki's tiles leave black are drawn at build time from the
game's own map data, read from the local game cache with RuneLite's cache library
(https://github.com/runelite/runelite, BSD 2-Clause) by the build tools. The picture content is
otherwise as the wiki serves it.

An index of which map squares exist is read from mejrs's public cartography repository
(https://github.com/mejrs/layers_osrs) by the build tools, so the build does not ask the wiki about squares
that have no tile. Only that listing is read. None of that repository's imagery is used.

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Map icon pictures
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
What is bundled: the small pictures the game's own world map draws for its icons (banks, altars, anvils, dungeon entrances,
the travel arrow and so on), 135 of them, in one atlas, gameicons/atlas.png, and the list of where the game puts each icon
(inside map-elements.json). The atlas also holds one picture that is RuneLite's blue fairy ring icon (BSD 2-Clause, from the
World Map plugin's resources), which is how RuneLite draws a fairy ring.

Where from: the game's own cache, read once at build time by the build tools with RuneLite's cache library
(https://github.com/runelite/runelite, BSD 2-Clause), the same way the map's text labels are.

Licence: the pictures are the Jagex sprites of the map icons, content of Jagex Limited, used here under Jagex's Fan Content Policy
as the credit above says, and bundled like the icons copied out of RuneLite (below). No licence is claimed for them. They are
drawn by the plugin on top of the map and are not changed, except that a copy at a smaller size is made when the map is zoomed out.

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Wiki data
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
What is bundled: shop stock and prices, shop keepers, monster, NPC and ground item spawn locations, quest start
places and music track unlock spots, as short data files (spawns.bin, shops.json and similar), and monster
drop tables and pickpocket loot (drops.bin: item, quantity, rarity and combat level per monster, with the item ids,
from the wiki's drop, item and monster tables), and what agility courses, minigames, some dungeons, farming patches and
transport networks ask of the player (requirements.json and the requires fields: levels, quests, quest points, checked
against the wiki's pages). They were extracted at build time from the Old School RuneScape Wiki
(https://oldschool.runescape.wiki) by the build tools. Names, numbers, coordinates and short place labels are
kept. Passages of the wiki's prose are not.

Licence: the wiki says its text is available under the Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial-ShareAlike 3.0
licence (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/3.0/), with additional terms. Much of this data is facts
about the game, and whether the wiki's licence applies to bare facts is not settled here. It is credited and
treated as if the licence applies: attribution to the wiki, no commercial use, and anyone who redistributes
these data files should keep this credit and the same licence for them. The data was reshaped into JSON (the drop
tables into a compact binary file, with Deadman Mode only tables and drops left out) and snapped to the map's icons.
Its content was not otherwise changed. Item values shown next to drops are not bundled: they are RuneLite's own item
prices, read from RuneLite while the plugin runs.

